[interviewer · INTRO] Your client is Brew & Bean, a specialty coffee chain with 200 locations across the US. Over the past two years, their net profit margin has declined from 24% to 6%, even though revenues have grown 15% over the same period. The CEO has hired us to identify the root cause of this profit decline and recommend how to reverse it. How would you approach this problem?

Take a moment, then walk me through how you'd approach this.

[candidate · 1:30] Let me take a second to structure this.

So revenue is up 15% but margin collapsed from 24% to 6% — that's a huge drop, so this is almost certainly a cost story, but I want to confirm revenue quality first before I jump there.

I'd break it into two branches. On revenue: is the 15% growth coming from new store openings, more transactions per store, or higher prices? That matters because growth from new stores can carry heavy fixed costs before they mature. On costs: I'd split into COGS — coffee beans, milk, cups — versus store operating costs like labor and rent, versus corporate overhead.

One thing I'd want early: do we have menu-price history over those two years? Whether they've raised prices changes how I read both branches.

If we don't have it handy, I'll start on the cost side since that's where the 18-point swing most likely sits. Do we have a cost breakdown as a percent of revenue for both years?
