[interviewer · CLARIFY · 1:33] Hold both requests for a moment, we'll come back to them. First, you have revenue and cost branches. Is that structure MECE, and what, if anything, is missing?

[candidate · 3:03] Fair challenge. Revenue and cost is collectively exhaustive at the top level, since profit is just revenue minus cost, so technically nothing can fall outside it. But I think my sub-branches have a gap.

The metric we're given is net profit margin, not operating margin. So below the operating line there's interest expense, taxes, depreciation and amortization, and any one-time charges — impairments, lease write-offs, restructuring. If they've been opening stores aggressively, they may have taken on debt, and interest plus D&A alone could explain a chunk of that 18 points without a single operating cost moving. I didn't have that in my tree.

The other thing missing is a segmentation cut that sits across both branches — by store cohort, new versus mature, and by geography. Averages across 200 stores could hide a handful of bad locations dragging the system down.

So revised: operating revenue, operating costs, below-the-line items, and segment the first two.
