[interviewer · EXHIBIT · 4:41] Coffee beans were 25% of COGS two years ago. Raw coffee bean costs are up 40% over the past two years. Other input costs — dairy, packaging, and food — are up 37.5% over the past two years.

[candidate · 6:11] Let me run this. Two years ago COGS was 42% of revenue. Beans at 25% of that is 10.5 points of revenue; everything else is 75%, so 31.5 points.

Now inflate: beans 10.5 × 1.40 = 14.7. Other inputs 31.5 × 1.375 = 43.3. Total = 58.0 points of revenue. That's exactly the 58% you gave me.

Two takeaways. First, the entire COGS increase is explained by input price inflation — there's no residual, so volume per transaction, waste, and product mix are essentially unchanged. That rules out an operational sloppiness story.

Second, and this surprises me a bit: this isn't a coffee bean problem. Beans only contribute 4.2 of the 16 points. The other 37.5% inflation across dairy, packaging, and food contributes 11.8 points — nearly three times as much. If I'd anchored on "coffee prices spiked," I'd have chased a quarter of the issue.

So the real finding is broad-based input inflation that was never passed through to customers in two years of flat menu prices.
