[interviewer · EXHIBIT · 6:14] Okay. You've concluded there's no residual, which rules out waste and mix. Walk me through why a full match on percent of revenue rules those out, given that revenue itself grew over the same period.

[candidate · 7:44] Let me be more careful, because I think I overstated it.

Working in percent of revenue does neutralize volume. Prices are flat, so revenue growth is pure volume growth, and COGS is variable, so it scales with volume — both numerator and denominator move together. That part is fine; growth doesn't distort the ratio.

But the match only rules out waste and mix under assumptions I didn't state. On waste: the 40% and 37.5% are input price increases. If spoilage had also risen, cost per unit sold would have grown faster than input prices, and I'd have landed above 58. I came in exactly at 58, so waste looks flat — that one I'd defend.

Mix is weaker. I applied the inflation rates using the old 25/75 weights. If mix shifted toward higher-cost items, that effect could be hiding inside the match, or offsetting something else. Two offsetting errors land on the same number.

So: waste, reasonably ruled out. Mix, not ruled out — I'd want current beans-as-percent-of-COGS to confirm.
