[interviewer · BRAINSTORM · 9:18] Okay. You mentioned a price increase as the obvious lever. Which of these would you prioritize first, and what would you want to know about customers before pulling it?

[candidate · 10:48] I'd prioritize the price increase, and it's not close. Prices have been flat for two years while inputs rose 37–40%, so the entire gap landed on margin. Roughly, to restore COGS to 42% of revenue I'd need revenue per unit up about 38% — 58/1.38 ≈ 42 — which is too big a jump at once, but even a 10% increase moves COGS to about 53% of revenue and recovers 5 margin points. Nothing else on my list has that magnitude or that speed. Procurement savings take quarters to negotiate; packaging spec is maybe a point.

Before pulling it I'd want price elasticity of demand — specifically how much volume we lose per point of price. Related: what competitors have done with their prices over the same two years, since industry-wide inflation means rivals likely raised too, and our customers' income profile and visit frequency. Daily-habit customers at a specialty brand are usually less elastic than they fear.

I'd test in a few markets before rolling to all 200.
