[interviewer · RECOMMENDATION · 10:52] Understood. What's your recommendation to the CEO?

[candidate · 12:22] My recommendation: raise menu prices, starting with a tested increase of around 10%, and pair it with procurement and packaging actions to close the rest of the gap.

The reasoning. The margin fell 18 points, and 16 of those are COGS. My walk-through shows the entire COGS increase is explained by input inflation — beans up 40%, everything else up 37.5% — with no residual from waste. Critically, menu prices have been flat for two full years, so none of that was passed through. This is a pricing failure, not an operations failure. And it's broad-based: beans are only about a quarter of the problem, so a coffee-only fix would miss most of it.

A 10% increase takes COGS from 58% to roughly 53% of revenue, recovering about 5 margin points. Supplier consolidation, hedging, and packaging spec can add a few more over the following year.

Risks: volume loss if customers are more elastic than expected, and brand damage at a specialty positioning.

Next steps: elasticity test in 15–20 stores, competitor price benchmarking, and the current COGS mix split to confirm my mix assumption.
